    Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding prime counting function algorithms and their complexity:
    
    ## Historical Algorithms and Complexity
    
    The sieve of Eratosthenes, the most efficient known method until the early 19th century, has a running time of O(x log log x) operations.
    
    Legendre's formula (1830) uses the inclusion-exclusion principle to calculate the number of primes below x without enumerating individual primes and has a running time of O(∛x) space.
    
    In 1959, D. H. Lehmer extended Meissel's formula and improved the running time to O(x^(3/8)) space.
    
    In 1985, J. C. Lagarias, V. S. Miller and A. M. Odlyzko published a new algorithm based on Meissel's formula which has a lower runtime complexity of O(x^(2/3)/log x) operations.
    
    ## Polylogarithmic Complexity
    
    I found one recent paper proposing an O(x^(1/2)) algorithm: A method to compute π(x) with time complexity O(x^(1/2)) without the need to introduce the non-trivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function. However, this appears to be from 2020 and hasn't been widely adopted in modern implementations.
    
    ## Modern Implementations (2026)
    
    The search results do not contain specific information about polylogarithmic complexity algorithms for prime counting from 2026. The most advanced practical algorithms mentioned are improvements to the Deleglise-Rivat and Gourdon methods, which achieve complexities better than O(x^(2/3)) by modest constant factors, but still polynomial rather than polylogarithmic.
    
    
