Session complete. Two clean null results recorded.

**What I tried (fresh angles):**
1. **BBP-style digit-freeze for psi(x)** — Empirically tested whether the kth digit of psi(x) freezes with polylog zeros. It doesn't: zeros-per-digit grows ~10^d (geometric in digits), confirming the sqrt(x) information bound. Anomalous x exist but are pure Gaussian-tail events.
2. **Compressed-sensing via random zero subsets** — Tested whether random K-subsets of zeros recover psi(x) better than first-K. They are **4x worse**, because the 1/|rho_k| weighting concentrates magnitude on low-frequency zeros. Signal is dense in the zero basis, not sparse.

**Other brainstorm ideas considered but not coded:** hierarchical-multipole sieve (no quadratic kernel to compress), Galois descent for pi(x) mod q (already covered by S35 pseudorandomness findings), spectral low-pass smoothing (mathematically equivalent to test #1).

**No breakthrough.** Both nulls re-confirm the existing GUE-random-phases barrier. 2026 literature search returned no new prime-counting algorithm publications. Files: `experiments/wildcard/bbp_digit_freeze{.py,_results.md}`, `experiments/wildcard/random_zero_subset{.py,_results.md}`. Status updated: SESSION_INSIGHTS.md (+S54), CLOSED_PATHS.md (+2 entries), archive/sessions/session54_bbp_digit_freeze.md created. `.run_state` set to 23.

