    Based on the search results, here's what I found about the Hiary algorithm and the t^(4/13) complexity improvement for zeta function evaluation:
    
    ## Overview
    
    Ghaith Hiary presented new fast methods to numerically evaluate ζ(1/2 + it), with the fastest method having complexity t^(4/13+o_λ(1)) (where 4/13 ≈ 0.307).
    
    ## Complexity Improvement
    
    This t^(4/13) complexity improves by a noticeable margin on the previous "complexity bound" of t^(1/3+o_λ(1)). Prior methods included the Riemann-Siegel formula, Schönhage's method, and Heath-Brown's method, with complexities having exponents 1/2, 3/8, and 1/3 respectively.
    
    ## Technical Approach
    
    The third method (achieving t^(4/13) complexity) employs an algorithm developed by Hiary to compute cubic exponential sums with a small cubic coefficient. The basic idea applies Poisson summation to obtain a shorter exponential sum of a similar type, followed by an intervention that suitably normalizes the arguments of the new sum, then another application of Poisson summation, which yields yet a shorter sum.
    
    ## Further Improvement Potential
    
    To improve on the complexity exponent 4/13, one must lower the precomputation cost, and if certain modifications to the cubic sums algorithm are carried out, the t^(4/13) method could potentially be improved to t^(3/10) complexity.
    
    
