    160	a major open question in complexity theory. The 20+ pseudorandomness measures
    161	provide the strongest empirical evidence for this possibility outside of
    162	cryptographic constructions.
    163	
    164	## What This Does NOT Prove
    165	
    166	**This synthesis cannot prove that pi(x) mod 2 requires super-polynomial circuits.**
    167	The reason is fundamental, not a limitation of effort:
    168	
    169	1. **The Natural Proofs barrier (Razborov-Rudich 1997).** Any proof that a function
    170	   is pseudorandom in a "natural" (constructive, large) sense would break
    171	   cryptographic pseudorandom generators -- and is therefore as hard as proving
    172	   P != NP. All 20+ measures above are "natural" properties in the Razborov-Rudich
    173	   sense: they are efficiently computable on truth tables and satisfied by a large
    174	   fraction of functions.
    175	
    176	2. **Pseudorandom does NOT mean hard.** There exist functions that pass every
    177	   efficient statistical test yet have small circuits (this is exactly what
    178	   cryptographic PRGs achieve, assuming one-way functions exist). Conversely,
    179	   there could exist structure in pi(x) that is invisible to our measures but
    180	   exploitable by a circuit.
    181	
    182	3. **The measures are empirical at finite N.** All experiments were conducted
    183	   at N <= 20 (x <= 10^6). Asymptotic behavior could differ. However, the
    184	   consistency of scaling laws across all tested N values makes a qualitative
    185	   change unlikely.
    186	
    187	4. **Circuit complexity is not captured by any single measure.** BDD size,
    188	   communication rank, approximate degree, and PTF degree each give lower bounds
    189	   in their respective models, but none directly implies circuit lower bounds
    ... (14 more lines)
